On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 00:45:49 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Is there any reason this should be done with IMAP specifically? I think
> all we care about is fetching the mail and indexing it. That could be
> done with any number of protocols.
>
> Not saying we shouldn't... I just want to understand the value that IMAP
> brings to this specific situation.

The reason in the spec comes from you, in "p7x mail relay jail" [1]:
the design should not be coupled to beastie, and agents should be
able to move to remote hosts. That rules out a nullfs mount. It does
not by itself pick IMAP over another network protocol, so here is
what IMAP brings, and the one alternative I think is a real contender.

What IMAP brings here:

- Read-only is enforced by the protocol server. Dovecot's ACL grants
  only list and read, and Dovecot cannot write the maildir anyway.
  A client gets mail and nothing else; it has no shell and no
  filesystem view.
- Incremental fetch is built in. mbsync tracks UIDs, so each fetch
  moves only new messages, with no state for us to write.
- It is the protocol every mail tool speaks, so a future client
  (Emacs, another agent, a phone) needs no special path.
- No accounts or keys to manage. Loopback is the boundary today; a
  dedicated address with pf later, as you said.

The cost is a daemon, Dovecot, and its config: about 30 lines, plus
the vmail user.

The alternative is rsync over ssh, with rrsync -ro on the lab-mail
side restricting each key to read-only access to the maildir. It
drops Dovecot and also fetches incrementally. But it needs sshd in
lab-mail, an account and a key per client, and its read-only
guarantee comes from a forced command instead of a protocol that
cannot express a write. Polling a git-backed maildir, as this list
does, is a third option, but it needs a commit job in lab-mail and
gives no better access control.

My recommendation (inference, not tested against rsync): keep IMAP.
For one read-only folder it is the narrowest interface, and it is
the one clients already speak. If you would rather drop the daemon,
I will rewrite the spec around rsync and rrsync before v9.

> Operator should install data dir.

Agreed; the dovecot dir moves to the operator's setup block too, as
I answered on 2/6.

